1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,080
Hello and welcome to the video. In this video guide we will show you how to deploy Burp Suite

2
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:15,920
Enterprise Edition on Kubernetes. You can deploy Burp Suite Enterprise Edition to any x86-based

3
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:21,440
cluster that meets the prerequisites for installation. For information on what we are

4
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:25,920
able to support you with when installing Burp Suite Enterprise Edition on Kubernetes,

5
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:32,560
please see our Support Scope for Kubernetes Deployments page. If you are moving an existing

6
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:37,680
installation to a Kubernetes deployment, it is important to note that each Burp Suite Enterprise

7
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:42,480
Edition license corresponds to a single running instance of Burp Suite Enterprise Edition.

8
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:48,800
Therefore you will need to migrate or decommission your existing setup in order to reuse an existing

9
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:52,080
Burp Suite Enterprise Edition license with a Kubernetes deployment.

10
00:00:52,080 --> 00:01:00,480
Deploying from scratch. In this section of the video we will explain how to deploy Burp Suite

11
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:06,240
Enterprise Edition to Kubernetes as a fresh installation. There are four main steps to

12
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:11,360
deploy Burp Suite Enterprise Edition to Kubernetes from scratch, that is without migrating any data

13
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:19,360
from an existing deployment. Step 1, set up your Kubernetes cluster. Step 2, install the application.

14
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:29,040
Step 3, configure the application. Step 4, activate your license. In this video we will run through

15
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:35,200
Step 1 and Step 2, running through the prerequisites and set up your cluster, along with how to install

16
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:41,200
the application using the Helm chart. You will then be ready to move on to Step 3, configure the application.

17
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:45,840
Step 1, set up your Kubernetes cluster.

18
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:50,880
Step 2, set up your Kubernetes cluster. Before deploying Burp Suite Enterprise Edition using the Helm chart,

19
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:55,040
you will first need an x86-based cluster that meets the following requirements.

20
00:01:55,040 --> 00:02:00,160
Step 2, an ingress solution. For example, a load balancer or ingress controller.

21
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:03,840
Step 3, set up your Kubernetes controller. In this example we have an AWS load balancer.

22
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:08,160
Step 4, set up your Kubernetes cluster. A persistent volume and a persistent volume claim.

23
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:12,640
Step 4, set up your Kubernetes cluster. The persistent volume should be simultaneously accessible to all nodes

24
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:18,400
available to the namespace. The persistent volume claim should be created in the same namespace as you

25
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:25,200
plan on deploying the application. Optionally, you may wish to take advantage of auto-scaling,

26
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:28,400
which will allow the cluster nodes to be scaled based on your scanning demand.

27
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:32,320
The cluster auto-scaler must be installed and enabled in this case.

28
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:38,640
As well as these Kubernetes requirements, please note that you will also need a suitable external database.

29
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:42,720
database. Please see our database requirements documentation for more details.

30
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:50,080
For customers that don't yet have a suitable cluster setup, we have an AWS reference template

31
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:56,640
available on a public GitHub repo. This reference template is intended as an example way of working,

32
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:01,680
therefore you may wish to fork it to your own repository and customize it to suit your own needs.

33
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:07,040
The reference template includes all of the prerequisites mentioned previously,

34
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:12,160
including auto-scaling. It provisions a PostgreSQL database as well for convenience.

35
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:18,240
We have used our AWS reference template to deploy and set up our cluster ready for deploying

36
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:21,280
BurpSuite Enterprise Edition in the next section of this video.

37
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:27,200
Please note that while we offer full support for Kubernetes deployments of BurpSuite Enterprise Edition,

38
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:30,960
we are unable to offer support to your underlying Kubernetes infrastructure.

39
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:35,680
We have used our software to install the application. This includes using and customizing any of our reference templates.

40
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:42,720
You can find more information on the SupportScope for Kubernetes deployments page in our documentation.

41
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:46,400
Step 2. Install the application.

42
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:51,680
BurpSuite Enterprise Edition uses Helm to manage installation and configuration.

43
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:57,360
In order to install the application, you first need to download the Helm chart from our releases page.

44
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:02,800
Once you have selected the version of BurpSuite Enterprise Edition that you wish to install,

45
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:07,200
you will need to select Kubernetes from the drop-down list and click Download.

46
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:10,400
This will download a zip file containing the Helm chart.

47
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:15,600
Once the chart is downloaded, unpack it into a directory of your choice.

48
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:20,000
Note that you will need the name of this directory when running commands against the chart.

49
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:25,040
Within the Helm chart, you will find a file called values.yaml.

50
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:29,840
This file contains the default values that will be passed to the Helm chart when you install it.

51
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:33,120
You can customize these values to suit your needs.

52
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:36,320
Please contact our support team if you need any additional guidance.

53
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:43,120
If you wish to use an Oracle database, you will need to manually enable Oracle Support by modifying the values file.

54
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:48,160
To do this, simply change Support Oracle to True.

55
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:53,360
Please note that while it is possible to fully customize the Helm chart,

56
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:57,520
we are unable to offer support in this process beyond customizing the values file.

57
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:03,120
For more information on this, please see the SupportScope for Kubernetes deployments page.

58
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:07,600
Before you install Burp Suite Enterprise Edition using the Helm chart,

59
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:12,720
make sure that you know or have created the namespace that you want to use for the deployment

60
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:15,600
and that your persistent volume claim is in this namespace.

61
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:20,000
We are going to use the namespace BSEE in this example.

62
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:23,520
To install the application, we're going to use the Helm command.

63
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:28,160
We're going to use "-n to specify the namespace we wish to deploy to."

64
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:31,200
So in our case, that's going to be BSEE.

65
00:05:32,640 --> 00:05:34,240
Followed by the name for the deployment.

66
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:37,600
I'm going to use BSEE-deployment.

67
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:41,520
Followed by the path and name of the Helm chart.

68
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:45,200
So this is the directory that we unzipped the chart to earlier.

69
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:49,360
Here you can see the installation is complete.

70
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:53,200
As I have used an AWS reference template,

71
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:56,640
I have an address for my AWS load balancer.

72
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:00,960
If you do not have an address here, once the installation is complete,

73
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:04,800
you will need to extract your Burp Suite Enterprise Edition external IP address

74
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:06,640
in order to access the application.

75
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:15,760
To do so, you will need to run kubectl, get services, minus n, and then your namespace.

76
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:22,240
This command details all of the services in the namespace, including their external IP addresses.

77
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:27,520
Ultimately, the address you use to connect to Burp Suite Enterprise Edition will depend on

78
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:29,600
the ingress solution you have chosen to use.

79
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:36,080
If you are using an alternative ingress solution, you can point it at the service for the web server.

80
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:40,320
In my case, as I am using the AWS load balancer,

81
00:06:40,320 --> 00:06:44,480
I'm going to use the web server service and extract the external IP address.

82
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:49,440
I can then use this address to connect to the Burp Suite Enterprise web console.

83
00:06:50,720 --> 00:06:56,240
You may have previously had a Kubernetes deployment of Burp Suite Enterprise Edition with a customized values file.

84
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:00,960
If so, you need to use the same values file with your new installation.

85
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:08,000
To specify the values file, add the "-f values file argument to the install command.

86
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:19,040
For example, helm install, minus n, our namespace, our deployment name, our helm chart,

87
00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:23,760
minus f, and our values file.

88
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:30,000
Now that the installation is complete, you are ready to configure the application.

89
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:37,200
You may need to wait a few minutes for the pods and services to start before connecting to the web console for the first time.

90
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:42,080
Seeing a restart on the web server pod is perfectly normal after installation.

91
00:07:42,080 --> 00:07:48,720
As there are potential security implications to leaving the application in an unconfigured state,

92
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:52,160
we recommend completing the rest of the configuration as soon as possible.

93
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:55,520
We hope you have found this video useful.

94
00:07:56,320 --> 00:07:59,440
If you have any questions, please reach out to our support team.

